  "prediction": "**War-Related and International Emergency Funding: A Review of Congressional Oversight Issues**\n\nSince the 9/11 terrorist attacks, Congress has appropriated approximately $2 trillion in discretionary budget authority for war-related and international emergency or contingency-designated activities. This funding has been used to support the U.S. government's response to the 9/11 attacks and other related international affairs activities. The majority of this funding, $1.8 trillion, has been allocated to the Department of Defense (DOD), with $154 billion going to the Department of State and U.S. Agency for International Development (USAID), and $3 billion to the Department of Homeland Security (DHS) and Coast Guard.\n\nThe Overseas Contingency Operations/Global War on Terrorism (OCO/GWOT) designation has been used to exempt funding from the Budget Control Act (BCA) limits on discretionary spending. This designation has been used to fund activities that were previously considered part of the base budget, such as equipment purchases and personnel costs. The use of OCO funding has been criticized as a \"slush fund\" for military and foreign affairs spending unrelated to contingency operations.\n\nThe report highlights the complexities of OCO funding, including the lack of clear definitions and the use of OCO funds for base budget requirements. The report also notes that the number of U.S. troops in Afghanistan, Iraq, and Syria has decreased significantly since 2008, but OCO funding has not decreased at the same rate. The report suggests that the use of OCO funding has created uncertainty in defense planning and has undermined budget controls.\n\nThe report recommends that Congress consider transitioning longer-term OCO expenses to the base budget, which could be achieved through a combination of increased budget caps, targeted cuts in inefficient Defense programs, and increased revenues. The report also notes that the National Defense Strategy Commission has recommended that Congress eliminate the final two years of caps under the BCA and gradually integrate OCO spending back into the base Pentagon budget.\n\nOverall, the report highlights the need for greater transparency and accountability in OCO funding and the importance of considering the long-term implications of relying on OCO funding to support U.S. military operations.",
